          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How do I know whether a three-character category can stand on its own as a usable code?",
          "answer": "A three-character category is complete only if the classification gives it no further subdivisions. The passages show how this works in ICD-10-CM, the US version used for diagnosis coding, but they don't give a full rule for checking every category.\n\n- **Most categories need more characters.** The coding conventions document says the three-character form is the category, and characters four to six add detail such as cause, site, severity, or laterality. Its example is E11.9, which has a decimal point because it runs past three characters.\n- **I10 is the exception.** The hypertension document says I10, essential hypertension, is complete at three characters. It takes no further characters, so asking for more specificity on it can't be satisfied by the classification.\n- **Subdivided categories show the pattern.** The COPD document says J44's fourth character records whether a complication is present, and it lists subdivisions including an unspecified code for a stable presentation. The type 2 diabetes document says E11 is split into complication subcategories.\n- **US and WHO versions differ.** The US version extends the WHO ICD-10 structure and subdivides many three-character categories further. A three-character code that looks complete in a WHO reference may have more characters in the US version.\n\nThe passages don't list which three-character categories are complete, and they don't describe how to look up the full code set. To confirm whether a specific code is billable, check the current official ICD-10-CM code tables published by the National Center for Health Statistics, or ask a certified medical coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
